window.DD_FULL = window.DD_FULL || {}; window.DD_FULL["satit-1"] = {"questions":[{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Choosing a suitable response in a dialogue","th":"การเลือกคำตอบที่เหมาะสมในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Thank you so much for helping me carry these boxes.\nB: ______","choices":["Same to you.","Yes, please.","You're welcome.","Excuse me."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'You're welcome' is the normal reply to 'thank you'. 'Yes, please' accepts an offer, 'Same to you' returns a wish such as 'Have a nice day', and 'Excuse me' gets attention or apologises.","th":"You're welcome เป็นคำตอบเมื่อมีคนขอบคุณ ส่วน Yes, please ใช้ตอบรับข้อเสนอ Same to you ใช้ตอบคำอวยพร และ Excuse me ใช้ขอโทษหรือเรียกความสนใจ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Answering a question about payment","th":"การตอบคำถามเรื่องวิธีชำระเงิน"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"Shop assistant: That's 850 baht altogether. How would you like to pay?\nCustomer: ______","choices":["I'd like a large one.","By credit card, please.","Every Monday.","It costs 850 baht."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'How would you like to pay?' asks for a method of payment, so 'By credit card' answers it. 'Every Monday' answers 'how often', the price has already been given, and 'a large one' answers a question about size.","th":"How would you like to pay? ถามวิธีชำระเงิน จึงตอบว่า By credit card ส่วน Every Monday ตอบคำถามเรื่องความถี่ ราคาบอกไปแล้ว และ a large one ตอบเรื่องขนาด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Accepting an apology","th":"การตอบรับคำขอโทษ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I'm sorry I'm late. The traffic was terrible.\nB: ______","choices":["You're welcome.","Don't worry. We've only just started.","It doesn't matter for me.","Me too. I'm sorry."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A apologises, so B should accept the apology kindly: 'Don't worry. We've only just started.' 'You're welcome' answers thanks, not an apology; 'Me too' makes no sense because B was not late; 'It doesn't matter for me' is a common Thai-learner mistake — we say 'It doesn't matter'.","th":"A ขอโทษที่มาสาย B ควรตอบรับคำขอโทษอย่างสุภาพ คือ Don't worry. We've only just started. ส่วน You're welcome ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ Me too ไม่สมเหตุสมผลเพราะ B ไม่ได้มาสาย และ It doesn't matter for me เป็นการใช้ผิดที่ผู้เรียนไทยพบบ่อย ที่ถูกคือ It doesn't matter"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Showing sympathy and offering help","th":"การแสดงความเห็นใจและเสนอความช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I failed my maths test again, even though I studied for hours.\nB: ______","choices":["That's a shame. Maybe we could study together next time?","Don't mention it.","I'm glad to hear that.","Congratulations! You must be very proud."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"A is upset about bad news, so B should show sympathy and offer help: 'That's a shame. Maybe we could study together…'. Congratulating or saying 'I'm glad' would be rude here, and 'Don't mention it' is a reply to thanks.","th":"A กำลังเสียใจกับข่าวร้าย B ควรแสดงความเห็นใจและเสนอความช่วยเหลือ คือ That's a shame. Maybe we could study together… การแสดงความยินดีหรือบอกว่าดีใจถือว่าไม่เหมาะสม และ Don't mention it ใช้ตอบคำขอบคุณ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Using the following line to choose a response","th":"การใช้ประโยคที่ตามมาเลือกคำตอบ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Shall we go to the beach this weekend?\nB: ______ The weather forecast says it will rain all weekend.","choices":["I'm not sure that's a good idea.","Sure, I'll bring my sunglasses.","What a great idea!","Yes, let's go right now."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"B's second sentence (rain all weekend) is a reason NOT to go, so B must be politely doubtful: 'I'm not sure that's a good idea.' Agreeing enthusiastically or bringing sunglasses contradicts the forecast, and 'right now' ignores 'this weekend'.","th":"ประโยคที่สองของ B (ฝนจะตกทั้งสุดสัปดาห์) เป็นเหตุผลที่ไม่ควรไป B จึงต้องแสดงความไม่เห็นด้วยอย่างสุภาพ คือ I'm not sure that's a good idea ส่วนการตอบรับอย่างกระตือรือร้นหรือการเตรียมแว่นกันแดดขัดกับพยากรณ์อากาศ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Refusing a request politely","th":"การปฏิเสธคำขออย่างสุภาพ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Could I borrow your ruler for a minute?\nB: ______ I'm using it for my art homework, but you can have it in five minutes.","choices":["No problem, take it.","Yes, you could.","Sorry, not right now.","Sure, here you are."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"B is still using the ruler and offers it 'in five minutes', so B is saying no for now: 'Sorry, not right now.' 'Sure, here you are' and 'No problem, take it' give the ruler immediately, which contradicts the next sentence. 'Yes, you could' is not a natural reply to 'Could I …?' (we say 'Yes, you can' or 'Of course').","th":"B ยังใช้ไม้บรรทัดอยู่และบอกว่าให้ยืมได้ในอีกห้านาที แปลว่าตอนนี้ยังให้ไม่ได้ จึงตอบ Sorry, not right now. ส่วน Sure, here you are และ No problem, take it คือให้ยืมทันที ซึ่งขัดกับประโยคถัดไป และ Yes, you could ไม่เป็นธรรมชาติเมื่อตอบ Could I …? (ควรใช้ Yes, you can หรือ Of course)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Responding to an offer of help","th":"การตอบรับหรือปฏิเสธข้อเสนอความช่วยเหลือ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Would you like me to help you with the washing-up?\nB: ______","choices":["Yes, I'd like to help you.","No, I don't like.","Yes, I would like you.","That's kind of you, but I'm almost finished."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A offers to help. A polite way to refuse an offer is to thank the person and give a reason: 'That's kind of you, but I'm almost finished.' 'Yes, I would like you' and 'No, I don't like' are incomplete (they need 'to help me' / 'no, thank you'), and 'Yes, I'd like to help you' wrongly offers help back to A.","th":"A เสนอจะช่วยล้างจาน การปฏิเสธอย่างสุภาพคือขอบคุณแล้วให้เหตุผล That's kind of you, but I'm almost finished. ส่วน Yes, I would like you และ No, I don't like เป็นประโยคไม่สมบูรณ์ และ Yes, I'd like to help you กลับกลายเป็น B เสนอช่วย A แทน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Understanding an idiom in conversation","th":"การเข้าใจสำนวนในบทสนทนา"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: social responses","th":"บทสนทนา: การตอบสนองในสถานการณ์ทางสังคม"},"passage":"","stem":"A: How was the science test?\nB: It was a piece of cake! ______","choices":["I finished it in twenty minutes.","I ate it all at lunch.","The teacher baked it for us.","I couldn't answer half the questions."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'A piece of cake' is an idiom meaning 'very easy', so B continues with evidence that the test was easy: 'I finished it in twenty minutes.' Not answering half the questions shows the test was hard, and the replies about eating or baking take the idiom literally.","th":"a piece of cake เป็นสำนวนแปลว่า ง่ายมาก B จึงพูดต่อว่าทำเสร็จในยี่สิบนาที ส่วนการตอบไม่ได้ครึ่งหนึ่งแสดงว่าข้อสอบยาก และคำตอบเรื่องกินหรืออบขนมเป็นการแปลสำนวนตามตัวอักษร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Using the next speaker's reply to infer the missing line","th":"การใช้คำพูดถัดไปอนุมานประโยคที่หายไป"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: Do you mind if I sit here?\nB: ______ My friend has just gone to buy a drink.\nA: Oh, sorry. I'll find another seat.","choices":["Not at all. Please sit down.","I'm afraid this seat is taken.","No, go ahead.","Of course not. It's free."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A's last line ('I'll find another seat') shows that B refused. 'I'm afraid this seat is taken' politely refuses and matches the friend who has gone to buy a drink. With 'Do you mind if …?', the answers 'Not at all', 'Of course not' and 'No, go ahead' all mean 'You can sit here' — the opposite of what happens.","th":"ประโยคสุดท้ายของ A (จะไปหาที่นั่งอื่น) แสดงว่า B ปฏิเสธ จึงตอบ I'm afraid this seat is taken ซึ่งสอดคล้องกับที่เพื่อนเพิ่งไปซื้อน้ำ ส่วนเมื่อถามด้วย Do you mind if …? คำตอบ Not at all, Of course not และ No, go ahead ล้วนแปลว่า นั่งได้ ซึ่งตรงข้ามกับสิ่งที่เกิดขึ้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"conversation","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Giving a suggestion that fits the reply","th":"การให้คำแนะนำที่สอดคล้องกับคำตอบ"},"topic":{"en":"Everyday conversation: requests, offers and opinions","th":"บทสนทนา: การขอร้อง การเสนอ และการแสดงความคิดเห็น"},"passage":"","stem":"A: I can't decide whether to join the drama club or the robotics club.\nB: ______\nA: Good idea. I'll ask the teachers if I can try one lesson of each.","choices":["I'd rather join the music club.","You should have decided last week.","Why don't you try both before you choose?","Both of them meet on Friday."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"A answers 'Good idea' and decides to try one lesson of each club, so B must have suggested trying both: 'Why don't you try both before you choose?' B's own preference for music, a criticism about last week, or a fact about the meeting day are not ideas that A could follow in this way.","th":"A ตอบว่า Good idea และจะขอลองเรียนชมรมละหนึ่งครั้ง แสดงว่า B แนะนำให้ลองทั้งสองชมรมก่อน คือ Why don't you try both before you choose? ส่วนความชอบชมรมดนตรีของ B คำตำหนิเรื่องสัปดาห์ก่อน หรือข้อมูลวันประชุม ไม่ใช่คำแนะนำที่ A จะทำตามได้แบบนี้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Antonyms","th":"คำตรงข้าม"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"The opposite of \"ancient\" is ______.","choices":["modern","quiet","huge","old"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Ancient' means very old, from long ago, so its opposite is 'modern' (new, of the present time). 'Old' is a synonym, not an opposite.","th":"ancient แปลว่า เก่าแก่ โบราณ คำตรงข้ามคือ modern (ทันสมัย) ส่วน old มีความหมายใกล้เคียง ไม่ใช่คำตรงข้าม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Matching a word to its definition","th":"การจับคู่คำกับความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"A doctor who takes care of sick animals is called a ______.","choices":["dentist","vet","pilot","chemist"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"A vet (veterinarian) treats animals. A dentist looks after teeth, a pilot flies planes and a chemist works with chemicals or sells medicine.","th":"vet (สัตวแพทย์) คือหมอรักษาสัตว์ ส่วน dentist คือทันตแพทย์ pilot คือนักบิน และ chemist คือนักเคมีหรือเภสัชกร"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing an adverb form","th":"การเลือกคำกริยาวิเศษณ์"},"topic":{"en":"Word formation","th":"การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"He spoke so ______ that everyone at the back of the hall could hear him.","choices":["clearness","clarity","clear","clearly"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The word describes HOW he spoke (the verb), so it must be an adverb: 'clearly'. 'Clear' is an adjective, and 'clearness' and 'clarity' are nouns.","th":"คำที่ขยายกริยา spoke ว่าพูดอย่างไร ต้องเป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์ คือ clearly ส่วน clear เป็นคุณศัพท์ และ clearness, clarity เป็นคำนาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Adjectives of feeling in context","th":"คำคุณศัพท์บอกความรู้สึกตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"After the six-hour hike, we were completely ______ and went straight to bed.","choices":["amazed","annoyed","excited","exhausted"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"A long hike followed by going straight to bed shows extreme tiredness: 'exhausted'. Being excited or amazed would not explain going straight to sleep, and nothing in the sentence suggests being annoyed.","th":"การเดินป่า 6 ชั่วโมงแล้วเข้านอนทันทีแสดงถึงความเหนื่อยมาก จึงใช้ exhausted ส่วน excited (ตื่นเต้น) และ amazed (ทึ่ง) ไม่ได้อธิบายการเข้านอนทันที และไม่มีอะไรบ่งชี้ว่า annoyed (รำคาญ)"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs in context","th":"กริยาวลีตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"The football match was ______ because of the heavy rain, so we stayed at home.","choices":["taken after","put on","called up","called off"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Call off' means cancel, which fits heavy rain and staying at home. 'Call up' means telephone or summon, 'put on' means organise a show or wear clothes, and 'take after' means resemble a family member.","th":"call off แปลว่า ยกเลิก เข้ากับเหตุฝนตกหนักและการอยู่บ้าน ส่วน call up แปลว่า โทรหาหรือเรียกตัว put on แปลว่า จัดการแสดงหรือสวมใส่ และ take after แปลว่า มีลักษณะเหมือนญาติ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Verb–noun collocations","th":"คำกริยาที่ใช้คู่กับคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"Please ______ attention to the safety instructions before the experiment begins.","choices":["make","do","pay","take"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The fixed expression (collocation) is 'pay attention to'. We do not say 'make', 'do' or 'take' attention.","th":"วลีที่ใช้คู่กันเสมอ (collocation) คือ pay attention to แปลว่า ให้ความสนใจ ไม่ใช้ make, do หรือ take กับ attention"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Synonyms in context","th":"คำพ้องความหมายตามบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"\"The instructions were so complicated that nobody could follow them.\" The word \"complicated\" is closest in meaning to ______.","choices":["badly printed","clearly written","very short","difficult to understand"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The result 'nobody could follow them' shows the instructions were hard to understand, which is what 'complicated' means. 'Clearly written' is the opposite, and length or printing quality are not mentioned.","th":"ผลที่ว่า \"ไม่มีใครทำตามได้\" แสดงว่าคำแนะนำเข้าใจยาก ซึ่งตรงกับความหมายของ complicated ส่วน clearly written มีความหมายตรงข้าม และประโยคไม่ได้พูดถึงความยาวหรือคุณภาพการพิมพ์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing a noun form","th":"การเลือกรูปคำนาม"},"topic":{"en":"Word formation","th":"การสร้างคำ"},"passage":"","stem":"The scientist made an important ______ about how the virus spreads.","choices":["discovered","discover","discovering","discovery"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After 'an important' we need a noun: 'discovery'. 'Discover' is a verb, and 'discovered' and 'discovering' are verb forms.","th":"หลัง an important ต้องเป็นคำนาม คือ discovery ส่วน discover เป็นกริยา และ discovered, discovering เป็นรูปกริยา"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Using a contrast clue to find meaning","th":"การใช้คำบอกความขัดแย้งหาความหมาย"},"topic":{"en":"Word meaning in context","th":"ความหมายของคำตามบริบท"},"passage":"","stem":"Fern is usually very ______, but yesterday she gave a speech in front of the whole school without looking nervous at all.","choices":["shy","rude","brave","lazy"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'Usually …, but yesterday …' shows a contrast: yesterday she was calm and confident, so usually she is the opposite — shy. 'Brave' would not contrast with speaking confidently, and 'rude' and 'lazy' have nothing to do with being nervous.","th":"usually … but yesterday … แสดงความขัดแย้ง เมื่อวานเธอพูดต่อหน้าทั้งโรงเรียนอย่างมั่นใจ ปกติจึงต้องตรงข้าม คือ shy (ขี้อาย) ส่วน brave ไม่ขัดแย้งกับการพูดอย่างมั่นใจ และ rude, lazy ไม่เกี่ยวกับความประหม่า"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"vocabulary","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Phrasal verbs with similar forms","th":"กริยาวลีที่มีรูปคล้ายกัน"},"topic":{"en":"Collocations and phrasal verbs","th":"คำที่ใช้คู่กันและกริยาวลี"},"passage":"","stem":"I ran ______ an old friend at the market yesterday. I hadn't seen her for years!","choices":["over","after","out of","into"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Run into somebody' means to meet them by chance, which fits not having seen her for years. 'Run out of' means to have none left (run out of milk), 'run after' means to chase, and 'run over' means to hit with a vehicle.","th":"run into somebody แปลว่า บังเอิญพบ เหมาะกับการเจอเพื่อนที่ไม่ได้เจอมาหลายปี ส่วน run out of แปลว่า หมด (เช่น นมหมด) run after แปลว่า วิ่งไล่ตาม และ run over แปลว่า ขับรถทับ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Modals of obligation","th":"กริยาช่วยแสดงความจำเป็น"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"You ______ wear a helmet when you ride a motorbike. It's the law.","choices":["needn't","might","mustn't","must"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'It's the law' shows an obligation, so 'must' is right. 'Mustn't' means it is forbidden, 'needn't' means it is not necessary, and 'might' only shows possibility.","th":"\"It's the law\" แสดงว่าเป็นข้อบังคับ จึงใช้ must ส่วน mustn't แปลว่า ห้าม needn't แปลว่า ไม่จำเป็น และ might แสดงความเป็นไปได้เท่านั้น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Quantifiers with uncountable nouns","th":"คำบอกปริมาณกับนามนับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"There isn't ______ milk left in the fridge.","choices":["any","a few","many","some"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Milk is uncountable, and in negative sentences we use 'any'. 'Many' and 'a few' are for countable nouns, and 'some' is normally used in positive sentences.","th":"milk เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ และในประโยคปฏิเสธใช้ any ส่วน many และ a few ใช้กับนามนับได้ และ some มักใช้ในประโยคบอกเล่า"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past perfect for the earlier past action","th":"past perfect สำหรับเหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนในอดีต"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"By the time we arrived at the cinema, the film ______.","choices":["has already started","was already start","had already started","already starts"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Two past actions: the film started BEFORE we arrived. The earlier past action uses the past perfect, 'had already started'. 'Has started' is present perfect and does not fit a past story; 'was already start' is not a correct form.","th":"มีเหตุการณ์ในอดีตสองเหตุการณ์ หนังเริ่มก่อนที่เราจะไปถึง เหตุการณ์ที่เกิดก่อนใช้ past perfect คือ had already started ส่วน has started เป็น present perfect ไม่เข้ากับเรื่องในอดีต และ was already start เป็นรูปที่ผิด"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Second conditional","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 2"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"If I ______ more free time, I would learn to play the guitar.","choices":["will have","would have","have","had"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Would learn' shows an unreal present situation (second conditional): If + past simple, would + verb. So 'If I had more free time'. 'Have' and 'will have' belong to the first conditional, and 'would' is not used in the if-clause.","th":"ประโยคใช้ would learn แสดงเงื่อนไขที่ไม่เป็นจริงในปัจจุบัน (conditional แบบที่ 2) โครงสร้างคือ If + past simple, would + V1 จึงใช้ had ส่วน have และ will have เป็นแบบที่ 1 และไม่ใช้ would ในอนุประโยค if"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Future passive","th":"passive ในอนาคต"},"topic":{"en":"Passive voice and reported speech","th":"ประโยค passive และ reported speech"},"passage":"","stem":"The new bridge ______ by local engineers next year.","choices":["will build","is building","will be built","has built"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The bridge does not build anything — it is built — and 'by local engineers' names who does the action, so a passive is needed. 'Next year' shows the future: will be + past participle = 'will be built'.","th":"สะพานเป็นสิ่งที่ถูกสร้าง และมี by local engineers บอกผู้กระทำ จึงต้องใช้ passive voice และ next year บอกอนาคต จึงใช้ will be + V3 คือ will be built"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Possessive relative pronoun whose","th":"ประพันธสรรพนาม whose"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses, agreement and connectors","th":"อนุประโยค ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา และคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"The girl ______ bicycle was stolen reported it to the police.","choices":["whom","whose","who","which"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The bicycle belongs to the girl, so we need the possessive relative pronoun 'whose' (= her). 'Who' and 'whom' refer to the person herself, and 'which' is for things; none of them can be followed directly by 'bicycle' to show possession.","th":"จักรยานเป็นของเด็กผู้หญิง จึงต้องใช้ whose ซึ่งแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของ ส่วน who และ whom ใช้แทนตัวบุคคล และ which ใช้แทนสิ่งของ ไม่สามารถแสดงความเป็นเจ้าของหน้าคำนาม bicycle ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Gerund after look forward to","th":"V-ing หลัง look forward to"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"I'm really looking forward to ______ you at the camp next week.","choices":["seeing","be seen","saw","see"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, not part of an infinitive, so it is followed by a gerund (-ing): 'looking forward to seeing you'. Using the base form 'see' is a very common mistake.","th":"ในวลี look forward to คำว่า to เป็นบุพบท ไม่ใช่ส่วนของ infinitive จึงต้องตามด้วย V-ing คือ seeing การใช้ see เป็นข้อผิดพลาดที่พบบ่อย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Stop + gerund vs infinitive","th":"stop + V-ing กับ to + V"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"Mint stopped ______ to her friend as soon as the teacher walked in.","choices":["to talk","talk","talked","talking"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"'Stop + -ing' means to end an activity; 'stop + to + verb' means to pause in order to do something. Mint ended her chat because the teacher arrived, so 'stopped talking' is right. 'Stopped to talk' would mean she began talking when the teacher came in, which does not make sense here.","th":"stop + V-ing หมายถึง หยุดทำสิ่งนั้น ส่วน stop + to + V หมายถึง หยุดเพื่อไปทำอีกสิ่งหนึ่ง มินต์หยุดคุยเพราะครูเข้ามา จึงใช้ stopped talking ถ้าใช้ stopped to talk จะหมายถึงหยุดเพื่อคุย ซึ่งไม่สมเหตุสมผล"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Question tags","th":"question tag"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"You've finished your homework, ______?","choices":["don't you","haven't you","have you","didn't you"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The statement is positive and uses the auxiliary 'have' ('You've finished'), so the tag is negative with the same auxiliary: 'haven't you?'. 'Didn't' and 'don't' use the wrong auxiliary; 'have you' is not negative.","th":"ประโยคหลักเป็นบอกเล่าและใช้กริยาช่วย have (You've finished) question tag จึงเป็นปฏิเสธและใช้กริยาช่วยตัวเดียวกัน คือ haven't you"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Third conditional with could","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขแบบที่ 3 กับ could"},"topic":{"en":"Conditionals and modals","th":"ประโยคเงื่อนไขและกริยาช่วย"},"passage":"","stem":"If you had told me about the problem earlier, I ______ you.","choices":["will have helped","could have helped","can help","could help"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'If you had told me' is about the past and did not happen (third conditional), so the result clause needs could/would + have + past participle: 'could have helped'. The other forms do not match a past, unreal condition.","th":"\"If you had told me\" เป็นเงื่อนไขในอดีตที่ไม่ได้เกิดขึ้นจริง (conditional แบบที่ 3) ผลจึงต้องใช้ could/would + have + V3 คือ could have helped รูปอื่นไม่สอดคล้องกับเงื่อนไขในอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Reported questions","th":"การรายงานคำถาม"},"topic":{"en":"Passive voice and reported speech","th":"ประโยค passive และ reported speech"},"passage":"","stem":"Mai asked me, \"Where do you live?\" Which sentence reports her question correctly?","choices":["Mai asked me where I lived.","Mai asked me that where I lived.","Mai asked me where did I live.","Mai asked me where do you live."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In reported questions the word order becomes a statement (subject before verb), 'do' disappears, the tense moves back (live → lived) and the pronoun changes (you → I). 'That' is not used before a question word.","th":"ในคำถามแบบรายงาน ต้องเรียงแบบประโยคบอกเล่า (ประธานก่อนกริยา) ตัด do ออก ถอยกาล (live → lived) และเปลี่ยนสรรพนาม (you → I) และไม่ใช้ that หน้าคำแสดงคำถาม"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"grammar","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Subject–verb agreement with neither … nor","th":"ความสอดคล้องของกริยากับ neither … nor"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses, agreement and connectors","th":"อนุประโยค ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา และคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"Neither the teacher nor the students ______ aware of the change to the timetable.","choices":["has been","were","was","is"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"With 'neither … nor', the verb agrees with the noun closest to it. That noun is 'the students' (plural), and the context is past, so 'were' is correct. 'Was', 'is' and 'has been' are singular.","th":"เมื่อใช้ neither … nor กริยาจะผันตามคำนามที่อยู่ใกล้กริยาที่สุด คือ the students ซึ่งเป็นพหูพจน์ และบริบทเป็นอดีต จึงใช้ were ส่วน was, is และ has been เป็นรูปเอกพจน์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Third-person -s in the present simple","th":"การเติม -s กับประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Every morning my sister [[walk]] to school [[with]] her friends, [[but]] today she [[took]] a taxi.","choices":["walk","with","but","took"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"'My sister' is third person singular and 'every morning' shows a habit, so the verb must be 'walks'. 'Took' is correct because 'today' describes something that already happened.","th":"my sister เป็นประธานเอกพจน์บุรุษที่ 3 และ every morning แสดงกิจวัตร จึงต้องใช้ walks ส่วน took ถูกต้องเพราะ today เล่าเหตุการณ์ที่เกิดขึ้นแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past simple with a finished time","th":"past simple กับเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: When we [[were]] in Ratchaburi [[last year]], we [[have visited]] the floating market [[with]] our cousins.","choices":["were","last year","have visited","with"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Last year' is a finished past time, so the past simple is needed: 'we visited'. The present perfect ('have visited') cannot be used with a finished past time.","th":"last year เป็นเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว จึงต้องใช้ past simple คือ visited ไม่ใช้ present perfect (have visited) กับเวลาในอดีตที่ระบุชัดเจน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past participle after have ever","th":"กริยาช่อง 3 หลัง have ever"},"topic":{"en":"Verb tenses","th":"กาลของกริยา"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: This is [[the most]] beautiful temple [[that]] I [[have ever]] [[saw]].","choices":["the most","that","have ever","saw"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After 'have ever' we need the past participle: 'have ever seen'. 'Saw' is the past simple form. 'The most' (superlative) and 'that' are correct.","th":"หลัง have ever ต้องใช้กริยาช่อง 3 คือ seen ไม่ใช่ saw ซึ่งเป็นช่อง 2 ส่วน the most และ that ถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Uncountable nouns","th":"คำนามนับไม่ได้"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: My father gave me [[some]] useful [[advices]] [[about]] how to [[prepare]] for the exam.","choices":["some","advices","about","prepare"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"'Advice' is uncountable in English, so it has no plural: 'some useful advice' (or 'some useful pieces of advice'). Thai learners often add -s because they count each idea.","th":"advice เป็นนามนับไม่ได้ในภาษาอังกฤษ จึงเติม s ไม่ได้ ต้องเป็น some useful advice (หรือ pieces of advice) ผู้เรียนไทยมักเติม s เพราะนับเป็นข้อ ๆ"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Using one linking word for contrast","th":"การใช้คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้งเพียงคำเดียว"},"topic":{"en":"Clauses, agreement and connectors","th":"อนุประโยค ความสอดคล้องของประธานกับกริยา และคำเชื่อม"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: [[Although]] it was raining heavily, [[but]] the children [[continued]] [[playing]] football.","choices":["Although","but","continued","playing"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"In English, 'although' and 'but' are not used together in one sentence (in Thai we say ถึงแม้ … แต่ …). Remove 'but': 'Although it was raining heavily, the children continued playing football.' 'Continue + -ing' is correct.","th":"ภาษาอังกฤษไม่ใช้ although คู่กับ but ในประโยคเดียว (ต่างจากภาษาไทยที่พูดว่า ถึงแม้ … แต่ …) ต้องตัด but ออก ส่วน continued playing ถูกต้องแล้ว"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"error","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"make + object + bare infinitive","th":"make + กรรม + กริยารูปเดิม"},"topic":{"en":"Verb patterns, comparison and quantifiers","th":"รูปแบบกริยา การเปรียบเทียบ และคำบอกปริมาณ"},"passage":"","stem":"Choose the underlined part that is incorrect: Because the essay was [[too]] short, the teacher [[made]] us [[to rewrite]] it [[before]] Friday.","choices":["too","made","to rewrite","before"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The pattern is 'make + somebody + base verb' without 'to': 'made us rewrite it'. Compare 'asked us to rewrite', which does take 'to'.","th":"โครงสร้าง make + กรรม + กริยารูปเดิม ไม่มี to จึงต้องเป็น made us rewrite it เทียบกับ asked us to rewrite ที่ต้องมี to"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Prepositions after verbs (turn … into)","th":"บุพบทหลังกริยา (turn … into)"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (1)?","choices":["onto","at","with","into"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"The pattern is 'turn something into something else' (change it). The rooftop was changed into a garden.","th":"โครงสร้าง turn A into B หมายถึง เปลี่ยน A ให้เป็น B ดาดฟ้าถูกเปลี่ยนเป็นสวนผัก จึงใช้ into"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Linking words for contrast","th":"คำเชื่อมแสดงความขัดแย้ง"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (2)?","choices":["However","Because","Moreover","Therefore"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The previous sentences give problems (doubtful teachers, a hot roof, no soil), and the next clause says the students did not give up — a contrast, so 'However'. 'Therefore' shows a result, 'Moreover' adds a similar point, and 'Because' cannot start this clause.","th":"ประโยคก่อนหน้ากล่าวถึงอุปสรรค (ครูไม่มั่นใจ หลังคาร้อน ไม่มีดิน) แต่ประโยคถัดมาบอกว่านักเรียนไม่ยอมแพ้ เป็นความขัดแย้ง จึงใช้ However ส่วน Therefore แสดงผล Moreover เพิ่มเติมข้อมูลไปทางเดียวกัน และ Because ใช้ขึ้นต้นตรงนี้ไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Adjective + preposition","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ + บุพบท"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (3)?","choices":["for","with","of","to"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The adjective 'responsible' is followed by 'for' + noun or -ing: 'responsible for watering'.","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ responsible ตามด้วยบุพบท for + คำนาม/V-ing จึงเป็น responsible for watering"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Relative which referring to a whole idea","th":"which ขยายความทั้งข้อความ"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (4)?","choices":["what","where","who","which"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"After a comma, a non-defining relative clause that refers back to a whole idea (turning waste into compost) uses 'which'. 'Who' is for people, 'where' for places, and 'what' cannot introduce a relative clause after a noun or idea.","th":"หลังเครื่องหมายจุลภาค อนุประโยคที่ขยายความทั้งข้อความก่อนหน้า (การทำปุ๋ยหมักจากเศษอาหาร) ใช้ which ส่วน who ใช้กับคน where ใช้กับสถานที่ และ what ใช้เป็นคำเชื่อมขยายคำนามไม่ได้"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Past simple in a narrative","th":"past simple ในเรื่องเล่า"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (5)?","choices":["has produced","produced","produces","will produce"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The story is set in the past ('Last year', 'said', 'cooked'), and 'after three months' is a finished past time, so the past simple 'produced' fits. The present perfect, present and future do not match the past narrative.","th":"เรื่องเล่าเป็นอดีต (Last year, said, cooked) และ after three months เป็นช่วงเวลาในอดีตที่จบแล้ว จึงใช้ past simple คือ produced ส่วน present perfect, present และ future ไม่สอดคล้องกับเรื่องเล่าในอดีต"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Choosing the adjective form","th":"การเลือกรูปคำคุณศัพท์"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (6)?","choices":["successful","success","succeed","successfully"],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"In 'so + ___ + that', the gap after the linking verb 'was' needs an adjective: 'so successful that'. 'Success' is a noun, 'succeed' a verb and 'successfully' an adverb.","th":"โครงสร้าง was so + ___ + that ต้องใช้คำคุณศัพท์ คือ successful ส่วน success เป็นคำนาม succeed เป็นกริยา และ successfully เป็นคำกริยาวิเศษณ์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"cloze","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Adjective + to-infinitive","th":"คำคุณศัพท์ + to + V"},"topic":{"en":"Cloze passage","th":"การเติมคำในบทความ"},"passage":"Last year, students at a school in Bangkok decided to turn an empty rooftop (1) ______ a vegetable garden. At first, many teachers were doubtful. The roof was hot, and there was no soil. (2) ______, the students did not give up. They researched how to grow plants in containers and asked a local farmer for advice. Every student in the club was responsible (3) ______ watering the plants on a different day of the week. They also collected food waste from the canteen and turned it into compost, (4) ______ reduced the amount of rubbish the school threw away.\n\nAfter three months, the garden (5) ______ more than twenty kilograms of vegetables. The canteen cooked them for lunch, and students said the food tasted fresher than ever. The project was so (6) ______ that two other schools have asked the club to help them start gardens of their own. \"We learned that you don't need a lot of space to make a difference,\" said the club's leader. \"You just need a plan and people who are willing (7) ______ together.\"","stem":"Which choice best fits blank (7)?","choices":["work","working","to work","worked"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"'Willing' is followed by a to-infinitive: 'willing to work together'.","th":"คำว่า willing ตามด้วย to + V1 จึงเป็น willing to work together"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"What is the passage mainly about?","choices":["How scientists study the eyes of sea animals","How an octopus changes its appearance to hide or protect itself","Why octopuses are colour-blind","Where the mimic octopus lives"],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Every paragraph explains part of the octopus's disguise: colour cells, skin bumps, copying other animals and how it may sense light. Colour-blindness, the mimic octopus's home and eyes are only small details.","th":"ทุกย่อหน้าอธิบายการพรางตัวของปลาหมึกยักษ์ ทั้งเซลล์สี ปุ่มบนผิวหนัง การเลียนแบบสัตว์อื่น และการรับรู้แสง ส่วนเรื่องตาบอดสี ถิ่นที่อยู่ของ mimic octopus และดวงตาเป็นเพียงรายละเอียดย่อย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"According to the passage, what happens when the muscles around a chromatophore pull?","choices":["The bag shrinks to a tiny dot.","The octopus becomes colour-blind.","Small bumps appear on the skin.","The bag of pigment stretches and the colour spreads out."],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 2 says: 'When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out.' The bag shrinks when the muscles relax, and the bumps are made by papillae, not chromatophores.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 2 กล่าวว่า เมื่อกล้ามเนื้อดึง ถุงสีจะยืดออกและสีกระจายออก ส่วนการหดเป็นจุดเล็กเกิดเมื่อกล้ามเนื้อคลายตัว และปุ่มบนผิวเกิดจาก papillae ไม่ใช่ chromatophores"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Word meaning from context","th":"การหาความหมายของคำจากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"In paragraph 1, the word \"texture\" refers to ______.","choices":["the size of the octopus","the temperature of the skin","how the surface of the skin feels or looks — smooth or rough","the smell of the water"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Texture is how rough or smooth a surface is. Paragraph 3 confirms this: the octopus raises bumps 'to copy the rough surface of a rock'.","th":"texture หมายถึง ลักษณะพื้นผิวว่าเรียบหรือขรุขระ ย่อหน้าที่ 3 ยืนยันว่าปลาหมึกยกปุ่มบนผิว \"เพื่อเลียนแบบผิวขรุขระของหิน\""}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"Why does the mimic octopus make itself look like a lionfish or a sea snake?","choices":["It wants to hunt lionfish and sea snakes.","Predators avoid animals that they believe are dangerous.","It cannot change its colour like other octopuses.","Lionfish and sea snakes are its closest relatives."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The passage says these animals are poisonous and that 'animals that might want to eat it usually keep away'. So the octopus copies dangerous animals to scare off predators. Nothing says it hunts them, cannot change colour or is related to them.","th":"บทอ่านกล่าวว่าสัตว์เหล่านี้มีพิษ และ \"สัตว์ที่อาจจะกินมันมักจะอยู่ห่าง\" จึงอนุมานได้ว่าปลาหมึกเลียนแบบสัตว์อันตรายเพื่อให้ผู้ล่าหนีไป ไม่มีข้อความว่าปลาหมึกล่าสัตว์เหล่านั้น เปลี่ยนสีไม่ได้ หรือเป็นญาติกัน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: science article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความวิทยาศาสตร์"},"passage":"The octopus is often called the master of disguise. In less than a second, it can change the colour, pattern and even the texture of its skin to match the rocks, sand or coral around it. For many years, scientists have tried to understand how an animal that is thought to be colour-blind can match its surroundings so well.\n\nThe secret lies in thousands of tiny cells in the octopus's skin called chromatophores. Each one is a small bag of coloured pigment — such as red, yellow, brown or black — surrounded by tiny muscles. When the muscles pull, the bag stretches and the colour spreads out, like a drop of ink on wet paper. When the muscles relax, the bag shrinks to a dot that is almost invisible. Because these muscles are controlled directly by nerves, the octopus can change its appearance almost instantly.\n\nColour is only part of the story. The octopus can also raise small bumps on its skin, called papillae, to copy the rough surface of a rock or the shape of seaweed. Some species go even further. The mimic octopus, found in the waters of South-East Asia, can change its body shape and the way it moves so that it looks like a poisonous lionfish or a sea snake. Animals that might want to eat it usually keep away.\n\nSo how does the octopus know which colours to use? Recent research suggests that its skin itself may be able to detect light. Scientists have found light-sensitive proteins, similar to those found in eyes, in the skin of some species. This does not mean that the octopus \"sees\" with its skin, but it may help to explain one of the ocean's most remarkable tricks.","stem":"What does the last paragraph suggest?","choices":["Light-sensitive proteins are found only in octopus skin.","Scientists are not yet sure how the octopus chooses its colours, but light-sensing skin may be part of the answer.","Scientists have proved that the octopus sees with its skin.","The octopus uses its eyes to see every colour clearly."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"The paragraph uses cautious words — 'suggests', 'may be able', 'may help to explain' — and says this 'does not mean that the octopus sees with its skin'. So the answer is still uncertain. The text also says the proteins are 'similar to those found in eyes', so they are not found only in octopus skin.","th":"ย่อหน้าสุดท้ายใช้คำที่ไม่ยืนยันแน่ชัด เช่น suggests, may be able, may help และบอกว่า \"ไม่ได้หมายความว่าปลาหมึกมองเห็นด้วยผิวหนัง\" แสดงว่านักวิทยาศาสตร์ยังไม่แน่ใจ และโปรตีนนี้ \"คล้ายกับที่พบในดวงตา\" จึงไม่ได้พบเฉพาะในผิวปลาหมึก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"e","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"Why did the writer first agree to volunteer at the shelter?","choices":["Because she enjoyed cleaning","Because Khun Dao asked her to help","Because her best friend was going too","Because she wanted to adopt a dog"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The first sentence says she agreed 'mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too'. She had not met Khun Dao yet, did not plan to adopt, and found the cleaning tiring.","th":"ประโยคแรกบอกว่าผู้เขียนตกลง \"เพราะเพื่อนสนิท ปลอย ไปด้วย\" ตอนนั้นยังไม่รู้จักคุณดาว ไม่ได้ตั้งใจรับเลี้ยงสุนัข และรู้สึกว่างานทำความสะอาดเหนื่อย"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Word meaning from context","th":"การหาความหมายของคำจากบริบท"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"What does Khun Dao mean when she says, \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles\"?","choices":["Cleaning and caring for the animals' needs comes before playing with them.","The animals do not like being touched.","Only clean animals can be cuddled.","The volunteers should clean their own homes first."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"She hands out gloves and buckets as she says it, and the volunteers then spend three hours cleaning. She means the basic care work is the priority; playing comes later.","th":"คุณดาวพูดขณะยื่นถุงมือและถังน้ำให้ และอาสาสมัครก็ทำความสะอาดอยู่สามชั่วโมง ประโยคนี้จึงหมายถึงงานดูแลพื้นฐานต้องมาก่อน การเล่นกับสัตว์มาทีหลัง"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"Why did the writer read aloud outside Mango's cage instead of trying to touch him?","choices":["Because she was not allowed to open the cage","To stop Mango from barking at other dogs","To let Mango get used to her without frightening him","Because she needed to finish her homework"],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Mango 'doesn't trust people yet' and hid in the corner. By staying close but not touching him, she let him get used to her calmly — and he slowly came closer. Mango never barked, and nothing says she was forbidden to open the cage or doing homework.","th":"มังโก้ \"ยังไม่ไว้ใจคน\" และหลบอยู่มุมกรง การนั่งอยู่ใกล้ ๆ โดยไม่แตะต้องช่วยให้มันคุ้นเคยกับผู้เขียนโดยไม่ตกใจ และมันก็ค่อย ๆ เข้ามาใกล้ มังโก้ไม่ได้เห่า และไม่มีข้อความว่าห้ามเปิดกรงหรือผู้เขียนทำการบ้าน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"Which of the following shows the correct order of Mango's progress?","choices":["leaned against her legs → took food from her hand → moved to the middle of the cage","took food from her hand → moved to the middle of the cage → leaned against her legs","moved to the middle of the cage → leaned against her legs → took food from her hand","moved to the middle of the cage → took food from her hand → leaned against her legs"],"answer":3,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 4 gives the days: day 5 he moved to the middle of the cage, day 8 he took chicken from her hand, day 12 he walked out and leaned against her legs.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 4 ระบุวัน: วันที่ 5 ขยับมากลางกรง วันที่ 8 กินไก่จากมือ วันที่ 12 เดินออกมาพิงขาผู้เขียน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: personal narrative","th":"การอ่าน: เรื่องเล่าจากประสบการณ์"},"passage":"When my mum suggested that I volunteer at an animal shelter during the school holidays, I agreed mainly because my best friend, Ploy, was going too. I imagined spending my days playing with cute puppies. I was wrong.\n\nOn the first morning, the manager, Khun Dao, handed us gloves and buckets. \"The animals need clean homes before they need cuddles,\" she said with a smile. For three hours we scrubbed floors, washed food bowls and carried heavy bags of rice. By lunchtime my arms ached, and I was beginning to regret my decision.\n\nIn the last cage sat a thin brown dog with one torn ear. Unlike the others, he did not bark or jump up when we walked past. He just pressed himself into the corner and watched us. \"That's Mango,\" Khun Dao explained. \"He was found by the road three weeks ago. He doesn't trust people yet.\"\n\nEvery day after that, I finished my jobs quickly so that I could sit outside Mango's cage. I didn't try to touch him. I just read my book aloud, sometimes for half an hour. On the fifth day, he moved from the corner to the middle of the cage. On the eighth day, he took a piece of chicken from my hand. On the twelfth day, when I opened the door, he walked out and leaned his whole body against my legs.\n\nAt the end of the holidays, a kind family adopted Mango. I cried when he left, but they were happy tears. Ploy says I talk about the shelter more than anything else now, and she is right. I went there expecting to have fun, but I came home having learned something much more valuable: trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.","stem":"What is the main lesson the writer learned?","choices":["Trust has to be built slowly through patience.","Dogs are better pets than cats.","Volunteering is always fun.","Friends should always do the same activities."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"The final lines state it directly: 'trust cannot be forced. It has to be earned, one small step at a time.' The story shows volunteering was hard work, not always fun, and cats or friendship rules are never discussed.","th":"ตอนจบบอกไว้ชัดว่า \"ความไว้ใจบังคับกันไม่ได้ ต้องค่อย ๆ สร้างทีละก้าว\" เรื่องยังแสดงว่างานอาสาเหนื่อย ไม่ได้สนุกเสมอ และไม่ได้กล่าวถึงแมวหรือการทำกิจกรรมเหมือนเพื่อน"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: history article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"Imagine carrying a bag of heavy metal coins every time you went shopping. For most of history, that is exactly what people had to do. Coins made of copper, silver or gold were valuable because the metal itself was valuable. However, they were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal.\n\nThe first widely used paper money appeared in China about a thousand years ago. Merchants in the province of Sichuan, who used heavy iron coins, began leaving their coins with trusted shops and receiving paper receipts in return. These receipts could be passed from person to person and exchanged for coins later. In the early eleventh century, the government took control of the system and began printing official notes.\n\nWhen the Italian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was amazed that people accepted pieces of paper as payment. Many Europeans who read his descriptions found the idea hard to believe. It was not until 1661 that a bank in Sweden issued the first banknotes in Europe.\n\nPaper money works only if people trust it. A note has very little value as paper; it is valuable because everyone agrees that it can be exchanged for goods. When governments printed too much money, that trust could disappear quickly. In some countries, prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes to buy a single loaf of bread.\n\nToday, the story is changing again. In many cities, people pay for coffee, bus tickets and even street food by tapping a card or scanning a code with their phones. Some experts predict that cash will one day disappear completely. Others argue that it will survive because it is private, simple and works even when the internet does not. Whichever side is right, one thing is clear: money is less about metal or paper than about trust.","stem":"According to the passage, what was the main problem with metal coins?","choices":["They could only be used in China.","They had no real value.","They were heavy to carry and easy to steal.","They were printed by the government."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 1 says coins 'were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal'. Their metal WAS valuable, and coins are not printed.","th":"ย่อหน้าแรกกล่าวว่าเหรียญ \"หนักเมื่อต้องพกไปไกลและถูกขโมยได้ง่าย\" ส่วนโลหะของเหรียญมีค่าจริง และเหรียญไม่ได้ถูกพิมพ์"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"m","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: history article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"Imagine carrying a bag of heavy metal coins every time you went shopping. For most of history, that is exactly what people had to do. Coins made of copper, silver or gold were valuable because the metal itself was valuable. However, they were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal.\n\nThe first widely used paper money appeared in China about a thousand years ago. Merchants in the province of Sichuan, who used heavy iron coins, began leaving their coins with trusted shops and receiving paper receipts in return. These receipts could be passed from person to person and exchanged for coins later. In the early eleventh century, the government took control of the system and began printing official notes.\n\nWhen the Italian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was amazed that people accepted pieces of paper as payment. Many Europeans who read his descriptions found the idea hard to believe. It was not until 1661 that a bank in Sweden issued the first banknotes in Europe.\n\nPaper money works only if people trust it. A note has very little value as paper; it is valuable because everyone agrees that it can be exchanged for goods. When governments printed too much money, that trust could disappear quickly. In some countries, prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes to buy a single loaf of bread.\n\nToday, the story is changing again. In many cities, people pay for coffee, bus tickets and even street food by tapping a card or scanning a code with their phones. Some experts predict that cash will one day disappear completely. Others argue that it will survive because it is private, simple and works even when the internet does not. Whichever side is right, one thing is clear: money is less about metal or paper than about trust.","stem":"How did paper money begin in Sichuan?","choices":["Marco Polo brought the idea from Italy.","Merchants left their coins with trusted shops and received paper receipts.","A bank in Sweden sent notes to China.","The government printed notes to replace gold coins."],"answer":1,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 2 explains that merchants with heavy iron coins left them with trusted shops and got receipts that could be passed on. The government only took control later, Marco Polo came centuries afterwards, and Sweden's notes came in 1661.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 2 อธิบายว่าพ่อค้าที่ใช้เหรียญเหล็กหนัก ๆ นำเหรียญไปฝากร้านที่ไว้ใจได้และได้ใบรับกลับมา ซึ่งส่งต่อกันได้ รัฐบาลเข้ามาควบคุมภายหลัง มาร์โค โปโลมาหลังจากนั้นหลายร้อยปี และธนบัตรของสวีเดนเกิดในปี 1661"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Inference from a text","th":"การอนุมานจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: history article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"Imagine carrying a bag of heavy metal coins every time you went shopping. For most of history, that is exactly what people had to do. Coins made of copper, silver or gold were valuable because the metal itself was valuable. However, they were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal.\n\nThe first widely used paper money appeared in China about a thousand years ago. Merchants in the province of Sichuan, who used heavy iron coins, began leaving their coins with trusted shops and receiving paper receipts in return. These receipts could be passed from person to person and exchanged for coins later. In the early eleventh century, the government took control of the system and began printing official notes.\n\nWhen the Italian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was amazed that people accepted pieces of paper as payment. Many Europeans who read his descriptions found the idea hard to believe. It was not until 1661 that a bank in Sweden issued the first banknotes in Europe.\n\nPaper money works only if people trust it. A note has very little value as paper; it is valuable because everyone agrees that it can be exchanged for goods. When governments printed too much money, that trust could disappear quickly. In some countries, prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes to buy a single loaf of bread.\n\nToday, the story is changing again. In many cities, people pay for coffee, bus tickets and even street food by tapping a card or scanning a code with their phones. Some experts predict that cash will one day disappear completely. Others argue that it will survive because it is private, simple and works even when the internet does not. Whichever side is right, one thing is clear: money is less about metal or paper than about trust.","stem":"Why did many Europeans probably find Marco Polo's descriptions hard to believe?","choices":["Paper had not been invented in Europe.","They did not believe Marco Polo had ever travelled.","They were used to money whose value came from the metal itself.","Chinese paper money was made of iron."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 1 explains that coins were valued because their metal was valuable. For Europeans who used only such coins, paper with almost no value of its own being accepted as payment would seem strange. The passage gives no evidence for the other reasons.","th":"ย่อหน้าแรกอธิบายว่าเหรียญมีค่าเพราะโลหะมีค่า ชาวยุโรปที่ใช้แต่เหรียญจึงคงแปลกใจที่กระดาษซึ่งแทบไม่มีค่าในตัวเองใช้ชำระเงินได้ บทอ่านไม่มีหลักฐานสนับสนุนเหตุผลอื่น"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Locating details","th":"การหารายละเอียดจากบทอ่าน"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: history article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"Imagine carrying a bag of heavy metal coins every time you went shopping. For most of history, that is exactly what people had to do. Coins made of copper, silver or gold were valuable because the metal itself was valuable. However, they were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal.\n\nThe first widely used paper money appeared in China about a thousand years ago. Merchants in the province of Sichuan, who used heavy iron coins, began leaving their coins with trusted shops and receiving paper receipts in return. These receipts could be passed from person to person and exchanged for coins later. In the early eleventh century, the government took control of the system and began printing official notes.\n\nWhen the Italian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was amazed that people accepted pieces of paper as payment. Many Europeans who read his descriptions found the idea hard to believe. It was not until 1661 that a bank in Sweden issued the first banknotes in Europe.\n\nPaper money works only if people trust it. A note has very little value as paper; it is valuable because everyone agrees that it can be exchanged for goods. When governments printed too much money, that trust could disappear quickly. In some countries, prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes to buy a single loaf of bread.\n\nToday, the story is changing again. In many cities, people pay for coffee, bus tickets and even street food by tapping a card or scanning a code with their phones. Some experts predict that cash will one day disappear completely. Others argue that it will survive because it is private, simple and works even when the internet does not. Whichever side is right, one thing is clear: money is less about metal or paper than about trust.","stem":"What happened when governments printed too much money?","choices":["People lost trust in it, and prices rose very quickly.","Banks returned to using iron coins.","People stopped buying bread.","Paper money became more valuable than gold."],"answer":0,"explanation":{"en":"Paragraph 4 says that when governments printed too much money, 'that trust could disappear quickly' and prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes for one loaf of bread. People still bought bread — it just cost far more.","th":"ย่อหน้าที่ 4 กล่าวว่าเมื่อรัฐบาลพิมพ์เงินมากเกินไป \"ความเชื่อมั่นก็หายไปอย่างรวดเร็ว\" และราคาสินค้าสูงขึ้นจนต้องใช้ธนบัตรกองใหญ่ซื้อขนมปังก้อนเดียว ผู้คนยังซื้อขนมปังอยู่ เพียงแต่ราคาแพงขึ้นมาก"}},{"kind":"m1","program":"satit","subject":"eng","lang":"en","format":"mcq","part":"reading","difficulty":"h","skill":{"en":"Identifying the main idea","th":"การจับใจความสำคัญ"},"topic":{"en":"Reading: history article","th":"การอ่าน: บทความประวัติศาสตร์"},"passage":"Imagine carrying a bag of heavy metal coins every time you went shopping. For most of history, that is exactly what people had to do. Coins made of copper, silver or gold were valuable because the metal itself was valuable. However, they were heavy to carry over long distances and easy for thieves to steal.\n\nThe first widely used paper money appeared in China about a thousand years ago. Merchants in the province of Sichuan, who used heavy iron coins, began leaving their coins with trusted shops and receiving paper receipts in return. These receipts could be passed from person to person and exchanged for coins later. In the early eleventh century, the government took control of the system and began printing official notes.\n\nWhen the Italian traveller Marco Polo visited China in the thirteenth century, he was amazed that people accepted pieces of paper as payment. Many Europeans who read his descriptions found the idea hard to believe. It was not until 1661 that a bank in Sweden issued the first banknotes in Europe.\n\nPaper money works only if people trust it. A note has very little value as paper; it is valuable because everyone agrees that it can be exchanged for goods. When governments printed too much money, that trust could disappear quickly. In some countries, prices rose so fast that people needed piles of notes to buy a single loaf of bread.\n\nToday, the story is changing again. In many cities, people pay for coffee, bus tickets and even street food by tapping a card or scanning a code with their phones. Some experts predict that cash will one day disappear completely. Others argue that it will survive because it is private, simple and works even when the internet does not. Whichever side is right, one thing is clear: money is less about metal or paper than about trust.","stem":"Which statement best expresses the writer's main point?","choices":["China and Sweden invented money at the same time.","Paper money was a mistake that caused rising prices.","The value of money depends on people's trust rather than on what it is made of.","Cash will certainly disappear in the near future."],"answer":2,"explanation":{"en":"The writer returns to trust throughout and ends: 'money is less about metal or paper than about trust'. The writer presents both views on the future of cash without choosing one, does not call paper money a mistake, and gives very different dates for China and Sweden.","th":"ผู้เขียนเน้นเรื่องความเชื่อมั่นตลอดบท และสรุปว่า \"เงินไม่ได้อยู่ที่โลหะหรือกระดาษ แต่อยู่ที่ความเชื่อใจ\" ผู้เขียนเสนอทั้งสองความเห็นเรื่องอนาคตของเงินสดโดยไม่ตัดสิน ไม่ได้บอกว่าธนบัตรเป็นความผิดพลาด และจีนกับสวีเดนเริ่มใช้ธนบัตรในเวลาที่ห่างกันมาก"}}]};
